      text: "Having been asked the question 'Where are you from?' roughly a thousand times, I have developed a repertoire of answers. The short one is 'London'. The honest one takes about twenty minutes and involves a map.\n\nI was born in Almaty to a Russian mother and a Korean-Kazakh father, and moved to England at the age of nine. My formative years, then, were split between two worlds that rarely spoke to each other. At home my upbringing was strict and unmistakably post-Soviet: shoes off at the door, soup before anything else, and a deep suspicion of anyone who smiled too much. At school I was learning to queue, to apologise for things that weren't my fault, and to pretend I had never eaten horse meat.\n\nFor years I assumed that one of these selves had to win. As a teenager I tried hard to assimilate, flattening my vowels and refusing to answer my mother in Russian in front of friends. Looking back, I can see that I was trying to escape being pigeonholed as 'the foreign girl'. It didn't work, of course. However perfect my accent, my surname gave me away, and my self-perception was constantly at odds with how others saw me.\n\nWhat changed things was, oddly enough, a trip to Kazakhstan in my mid-twenties. Expecting to feel that I had finally come home, I instead discovered that I was an outsider there too. My cousins teased me about my careful, old-fashioned Russian, and I didn't know any of the songs everyone sang at my aunt's birthday. Standing in that crowded kitchen, I realised that I did not belong fully anywhere, and that this was not a tragedy.\n\nSince then I have come to terms with my hyphenated identity. I no longer feel I need to choose a single allegiance or prove the authenticity of my roots to anyone. Identity, it seems to me, is not an inherent, fixed thing that you discover, like a birthmark. It is something you keep putting together from the pieces you are given: your ancestry, your language, the places that have moulded you, the people who claim you as kin.\n\nSo when someone asks where I'm really from, I try not to take offence. Usually they are simply curious. But I have stopped feeling obliged to give the twenty-minute version. 'It's complicated,' I say, 'and I rather like it that way.'",
