      minWords: 220, use: ["upbringing", "sense of belonging", "having", "come to terms with"],
      model: "Is our identity determined by where we happen to be born? Many people would instinctively say yes. After all, our birthplace usually decides our first language, our passport and the customs we grow up with. Yet, having met many people who have moved countries, I am convinced that the answer is far more complicated.\n\nOn the one hand, there is no denying that the place where we spend our formative years leaves a deep mark. Our upbringing shapes our tastes, our sense of humour and even the way we argue. A friend of mine who left Ukraine at thirty still cooks the dishes of her childhood every Sunday, and says that doing so gives her a sense of belonging that no new country can replace.\n\nOn the other hand, a birthplace is often an accident. My cousin was born in Germany while his parents were working there briefly, but he grew up in Kazan and has never felt remotely German. For him, identity is about language, family and friends, not a line in a document. Moreover, many people who emigrate as children end up feeling completely at home in their new country, having absorbed its values and habits.\n\nIn my view, where we are born is only the starting point. Identity is something we keep building throughout our lives, and many of us have to come to terms with belonging to more than one place. Rather than a weakness, this can be a real source of strength."
