      minWords: 220, use: ["connotation", "lost in translation", "literal", "subtext"],
      model: "What a Foreign Language Taught Me\n\nWhen I started learning English seriously at twenty-five, I thought it was simply a matter of memorising words and grammar rules. I was wrong. Five years and countless embarrassing moments later, I can say that learning a language has changed the way I see the world, and even the way I see myself.\n\nThe first lesson was that dictionaries can lie. Or rather, they tell only half the truth. A word may have a perfect literal equivalent, but its connotation can be completely different. When I described a colleague as 'ambitious', meaning it as a compliment, I was surprised to see her smile with obvious pleasure. In Russian the word often sounds rather negative; in English it is usually positive. Clearly, something had got lost in translation, though in the opposite direction from what I expected.\n\nThe second lesson was about subtext. British people, I discovered, rarely say exactly what they mean. 'Quite good' can mean 'disappointing', and 'with the greatest respect' often introduces an insult. At first this drove me mad. Now I find it fascinating, because it shows how much a culture values politeness and avoiding open conflict.\n\nFinally, English has made me more aware of my own language. I notice things in Russian that I never noticed before: how direct we are, how much we rely on tone. Learning a foreign language, it turns out, is also a way of discovering your own."
