      minWords: 230, use: ["handed down", "die out", "reinvented", "rituals"],
      model: "Traditions are often described as the glue that holds a society together. Yet in a world that changes faster every year, many people wonder whether time-honoured customs should be preserved exactly as they were, or whether they need to change in order to survive. In my opinion, the second view is far more realistic.\n\nThose who argue for keeping traditions unchanged do have a point. Customs that have been handed down for centuries connect us with our ancestors and give us a sense of continuity. If every generation alters them as it pleases, there is a danger that their original meaning will be forgotten. Some people also fear that commercialisation turns meaningful rituals into empty shows for tourists.\n\nHowever, history shows that traditions have always evolved. In Russia, for example, New Year celebrations borrowed many elements from Christmas in the Soviet period, and today they are cherished by almost everyone, religious or not. Similarly, Maslenitsa, which was once a pagan festival marking the end of winter, has been reinvented as a city holiday with concerts and food fairs. If it had remained a purely rural ritual, it might well have disappeared along with the villages where it was celebrated. It seems to me that customs which refuse to change are the ones most likely to die out.\n\nTo sum up, I believe that the essence of a tradition, the values and feelings it expresses, matters more than its exact form. A tradition that adapts does not lose its soul; on the contrary, that is how it stays alive."
