    text: "For many children of immigrants, the language their parents speak at home becomes something they understand but no longer use. Linguists call these young people heritage speakers, and their numbers are growing in every major city. We spoke to Dr Amara Osei, who has spent fifteen years studying families in which more than one language is spoken.\n\nIs it inevitable that the heritage language fades?\nNot inevitable, but very common. There is a well-known pattern: the first generation speaks the language of the old country, the second understands it but answers in the language of the host country, and the third often loses it altogether. Having grown up surrounded by English at school and online, children quickly realise which language carries status. Parents, many of whom are working long hours, simply don't have the energy to insist.\n\nDoes it matter? Surely English opens more doors.\nIt does open doors, of course. But losing a heritage language has costs that are easy to underestimate. When grandparents and grandchildren can't talk to each other properly, something breaks in the family. I've interviewed teenagers who described feeling like outsiders at family gatherings, unable to follow the jokes, nodding along without understanding. Several told me they felt they had been cut off from half of their identity.\n\nWhat can families do?\nThe most effective strategy is surprisingly simple: create situations in which the language is genuinely needed. Children are pragmatic. If they know that their grandmother really doesn't speak English, they will make the effort. Summer visits to the country of origin, weekend schools and even video calls all help. What doesn't work is pressure and shame. Correcting every mistake, or comparing a child with cousins back home, tends to produce exactly the opposite of what parents want.\n\nSome people argue that keeping the old language slows down integration.\nThat is a myth, and a persistent one. The research is very consistent: bilingual children do not fall behind in the long run; if anything, they tend to develop greater flexibility of thinking. The real risk is the opposite one, that children absorb the message that their parents' culture is something to be ashamed of.\n\nAre you optimistic?\nCautiously, yes. Attitudes have changed. Twenty years ago, parents were often told by teachers to stop speaking their language at home. That advice, which we now know to be wrong, is far less common today. And I meet more and more young adults who, having lost the language as children, are deliberately going back to learn it. They often say the same thing: 'I wanted to be able to talk to my grandmother before it was too late.'",
