    model: "Can a person ever truly belong in a country they were not born in? It is a question that millions of migrants ask themselves, often many years after they have unpacked their suitcases. In my view, it is possible to belong, but rarely in quite the same way as locals, and never without effort.\n\nThere are clear reasons why full belonging can seem out of reach. Language is the most obvious one. Even people who speak their new language fluently often find that their mother tongue remains the language of their emotions: they count, swear and dream in it. Accents, too, can mark someone as an outsider for decades. Furthermore, many adult migrants miss the shared childhood references, the songs, cartoons and jokes, that locals take for granted.\n\nOn the other hand, belonging is not only about the past; it is also about participation. Migrants who work, raise children, volunteer and make friends gradually build a sense of belonging that is just as real as that of people born there. I know several families who came to Germany in the 1990s, many of whom now say that they feel more at home in Berlin than in the towns where they grew up. Having lived there for thirty years, they no longer see themselves as guests.\n\nMuch also depends on the host country itself. Societies that accept that identity can be plural make it far easier for newcomers to feel welcome.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that becoming completely local in every sense may be impossible, but feeling genuinely at home is not. Belonging is something we build, not something we are simply born with."
